    "result_text": "Tests green, build succeeds.\n\n## Response to the review\n\nI agreed with the finding and fixed it. The duplication was real: my `Owner.sha256Hex` was a second copy of the same `MessageDigest` acquisition, hex loop, and verbatim `\"SHA-256 is required but not available\"` message that `ClinicServiceImpl.sha256Hex` already owned \u2014 and the service method's Javadoc explicitly claimed that logic lived \"in one place.\"\n\nI took the suggested additive route:\n\n- **New `util/Sha256.java`** next to `Soundex`/`PostcodeRules`, centralizing the `MessageDigest` call and the `NoSuchAlgorithmException \u2192 IllegalStateException` mapping. It exposes `hex(String)` (full 64-char lower-case digest) and `hexPrefix(String, bytes, upperCase)` (the prefix/case-toggle variant). `hex` is just `hexPrefix(input, 32, false)`, so there's a single digest routine.\n- **`Owner.getIdentityKey()`** now calls `Sha256.hex(...)`; the private copy and its crypto imports are gone. Identity-key derivation stays on the model (consistent with `getCheckDigit` etc.) with no model\u2192service dependency.\n- **`ClinicServiceImpl`** deletes its private `sha256Hex`; `computeHouseholdId` and `customerHash` call `Sha256.hexPrefix(...)` directly, and the three now-unused `java.security`/charset imports are removed.\n\nAcceptance behaviour is unchanged \u2014 `identityKey`, `householdId`, and the customer-code hash all produce identical output, confirmed by the passing suite (`Tests run: 2, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`).",
